[2425.652181, "o", "paid 0.01 NEAR  tx 7Xsr9VBuEHaYC8ibC46m4bXMvVx12c5EzVHxaX5S2wad\r\nLaing distal myopathy is caused by mutations in the DMD gene, which provides instructions for making dystrophin, an important protein that helps provide structural support within muscle cells (sarcoplasmic reticulum). Mutations in this gene result in the production of an abnormally short or nonfunctional version of dystrophin. Without functional dystrophin to help maintain the structure of muscle cells, muscles weaken and begin to break down over time. The severity of the condition can vary greatly among individuals with mutations affecting different parts of the DMD gene.\r\n"]
